    {
      "filename": "Celebrate The Return of Axum Obelisk (3170898463).jpg",
      "title": "File:Celebrate The Return of Axum Obelisk (3170898463).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Celebrate_The_Return_of_Axum_Obelisk_%283170898463%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celebrate_The_Return_of_Axum_Obelisk_(3170898463).jpg",
      "width": 1231,
      "height": 1691,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "A. Davey from Where I Live Now: Pacific Northwest",
      "description": "This is the ultimate \"deal toy.\"  It was on display in the lobby of our hotel in Axum, the Ramhai Hotel.  As the label indicates (see the close-up below), the object  celebrates the return to Axum of an obelisk -  an ancient funerary monument dating to the Axumite Kingdom -  looted by the Italian government in the 30s.\nAccording to Wikipedia,\n\"The Obelisk of Axum is a 1700-year-old, 24-meters (78-foot) tall granite obelisk, weighing 160 tonnes. It is decorated with two false doors at the base, and decorations resembling windows on all sides. The obelisk ends in a semicircular top part, which used to be enclosed by metal frames.\"   It was probably erected in the 4th century of the common era.  en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obelisk_of_Axum\nIf the Italian government couldn't keep Ethiopia, which it unsuccessfully attempted to colonize in the late 30s, at least it managed to hang on to a piece of stolen Ethiopian history - the Axum Obelisk - much longer than many would have liked.\nWhen Italian forces reached Axum in 1935, they found the Axum Obelisk in a ruined state, having been toppled and broken for centuries, perhaps longer.\nIn keeping with the Imperial Roman tradition of hauling loot from conquered nations to the capital city, Rome, the Italians, under official orders, cut the fallen obelisk into six pieces, hauled the sections overland manually to the Eritrean port of Massawa and loaded it onto a boat bound for Naples.\nThe reconstructed Axum Obelisk was raised in front of the Ministry for Italian Africa ( ! ) in Rome, across from the Circus Maximus, in October 1937.\nIn 1947, having lost both Ethiopia and WWII, Italy agreed to return the Axum Obelisk to Ethiopia.  A series of complicated delays then ensued, including claims that Ethiopia had relinquished its claim to the Obelisk.  At last, in 1997, the Italian President of the moment agreed to repatriate the Obelisk.\nThe first steps toward dismantling the Obelisk took place in Rome in 2003.  To make a very long story short, the obelisk returned to Ethiopia in 2005; reassembly of the Obelisk in Axum began in June 2008; and the restored Axum Obelisk was unveiled in September of the same year.\nFor much more information about the Axum Obelisk, visit Wikipedia at:\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obelisk_of_Axum",
      "dateCreated": "2007-11-01 07:56",
      "categories": [
        "Aksum Stele n. 2 (Obelisk of Axum-Roma)",
        "CC-BY-2.0",
        "Deal toys",
        "Files created by A.Davey",
        "Flickr images reviewed by File Upload Bot (Magnus Manske)",
        "Taken with Nikon D70"
      ],
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "obelisk-of-axum-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "sfe8",
      "smallPath": "_m/s/f/e/8/obelisk-of-axum-wp/celebrate-the-return-of-axum-o-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Celebrate The Return of Axum Obelisk (Detail) (3172365788).jpg",
      "title": "File:Celebrate The Return of Axum Obelisk (Detail) (3172365788).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Celebrate_The_Return_of_Axum_Obelisk_%28Detail%29_%283172365788%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celebrate_The_Return_of_Axum_Obelisk_(Detail)_(3172365788).jpg",
      "width": 2986,
      "height": 1972,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "A. Davey from Where I Live Now: Pacific Northwest",
      "description": "This is a photo of the base of the model that commemorates the Axum Obelisk's return to Ethiopia.\nOn the left, the base of the Axum Obelisk rests on a piece of dark green plastic shaped like the Italian Peninsula.  \nGiven the obelisk's outsized scale in relation to Italy, I suppose it's safe to say that part of the obelisk rests on Rome (where the obelisk spent 68 years), though the edge of the obelisk closest to the camera is nearer to Naples.\nEthiopia is represented on the right by a piece of red plastic in the shape of the country.\nThe white tusk-shaped marker running from Ethiopia to Italy bears the date 1929.  Presumably, this is the date the obelisk left Ethiopia for Italy.\nAbove, the yellow marker running from Italy to Ethiopia gives a date of 1997 which, we assume, is when the obelisk was repatriated to Ethiopia.\nBut wait - doesn't my description under the full-sized photo of the model say the obelisk left Ethiopia in 1937 and returned in 2005?\nIt does!  So how do we reconcile the difference?  It's easy.  Unlike most Western countries, Ethiopia doesn't use the Gregorian calendar.\nUnder the Ethiopian calendar, the year 1929 (which is the year on the white marker) corresponds to 1935 on the Gregorian calendar.  And, indeed, 1935 is when the Italian government carted the Axum Obelisk off to Rome.\nSimilarly, 1997 on the Ethiopian calendar corresponds to 2005 on the Gregorian calendar, the year the Axum Obelisk finally returned to Ethiopian soil.\n\nI wonder how many of these commemorative deal toys were produced.",
      "dateCreated": "2007-11-01 07:56",
      "categories": [
        "Aksum Stele n. 2 (Obelisk of Axum-Roma)",
        "CC-BY-2.0",
        "Files created by A.Davey",
